  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>TLV-TVM (Transports Littoral Varois) runs the shortest and most frequent service to Porquerolles, departing from La Tour Fondue on the Giens peninsula — a crossing of about 15 minutes. The company also serves Port-Cros and Le Levant from the port of Hyères.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>Dogs are accepted on board free of charge, provided they don't disturb other passengers or the crew. Once on the island, the rule changes depending on where you go: on <strong>Porquerolles</strong>, your dog can join you on a lead across almost the whole island, except on the three main beaches (Notre-Dame, Argent, Courtade) between 8am and 8pm from 1 April to 30 September. On <strong>Port-Cros</strong>, the rule is much stricter: dogs are only allowed in the village, between Fort du Moulin and the Saint-Joseph statue — not on the island's trails.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: for a proper walk with your dog, choose Porquerolles rather than Port-Cros — the latter isn't suited to walking dogs despite its wild look suggesting otherwise.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The Giens peninsula, with its salt-flats trail, is well suited to a walk before or after the crossing.</p>
    </div>

